  "overview": "The Due was Arduino's first 32-bit ARM board, built around Atmel's SAM3X8E and physically shaped like a Mega so existing Mega shields mostly fit. Its defining trait — and its most common beginner-destroying mistake — is that it runs at 3.3V logic with pins that are NOT 5V tolerant, unlike every classic AVR Arduino before it. Wiring a 5V sensor straight to a Due pin the way you would on an Uno or Mega can permanently damage it. In exchange you get a real floating-point unit, two true analog outputs (DAC), and a native USB port alongside the usual programming port.",
  "simpleSummary": "An early 32-bit Arduino, shaped like the Mega, for projects needing more speed and memory.",
  "specifications": {
    "microcontroller": "Atmel SAM3X8E, Arm Cortex-M3, 84 MHz",
    "operatingVoltage": "3.3V (NOT 5V tolerant)",
    "inputVoltageRecommended": "7-12V (via VIN)",
    "flashMemory": "512 KB",
    "sram": "96 KB",
    "digitalIOPins": 54,
    "pwmPins": 12,
    "analogInputPins": 12,
    "analogOutputPins": "2 (true DAC, not PWM-simulated)",
    "dimensions": "101.52mm x 53.3mm",
    "connector": "2x Micro-USB (native USB + programming port), plus barrel jack"
  },
